  "worked_solution": "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{-4}{{x}^{2}+7x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{-4}{x(x+7)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)=0$ has no solution. On the other hand, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=-7$ and $x=0.$ These asymptotes divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-7),\\,(-7,0),\\,(0,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-7,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)>0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-7,0\\,).\n$$",
